;; A clause macro's clauses after its first non-driver go to the end of the
;; driver group. A driver among them would open a level inside the macro's
;; place, and the drivers beside the macro would no longer step once for each
;; of its elements, so it is refused, naming the macro.
;;
;; EXPECT-ERROR: The clause macro 'in-nested'
;; EXPECT-ERROR: answers a driver or a (:subloop) after a clause that is not a driver

(import "../inc/clause_macros.bjo")

(defun (main args)
  (println (int->string {summing x (:for x (in-nested [[1 2] [3]]))}))
  0)
